node_modules/
dist-web/
dist-site/
desktop/www/
mobile/www/
# Generated per machine by `cap add` — and regenerated freely, which is why
# the mic permission is re-applied by a script rather than committed here.
mobile/ios/
mobile/android/
.ai-notes-test-tmp/
# CLAUDE CODE'S DIRECTORY IS IGNORED BY CONTENTS, NOT AS A DIRECTORY, so
# two things in it can be shared. Git cannot re-include a file whose
# PARENT is excluded, so `.claude/` would make every negation below
# inert -- `.claude/*` is what leaves the directory itself visible.
#
# What is shared: the PreToolUse hook that refuses `git checkout` against
# a dirty path, and the settings file that wires it. Those are a rule in
# CLAUDE.md turned into a mechanism, so they belong to the project rather
# than to whoever happened to write them.
#
# What stays ignored: settings.local.json (personal overrides, by
# design), and everything else Claude Code writes here per machine.
.claude/*
!.claude/settings.json
!.claude/hooks/

# Release signing secrets. The keystore must live OUTSIDE the repo; these
# entries are the backstop for the day someone creates one inside it and
# runs `git add -A`. A test asserts they are present AND that git really
# ignores a file at each of these paths -- an entry can be present and
# still not bite, which is the only failure mode a text check cannot see.
*.jks
*.keystore
key.properties

# ENVIRONMENT FILES AND CREDENTIAL MATERIAL. The keystore entries above
# were added as "the backstop for the day someone creates one inside the
# repo and runs `git add -A`" -- and .env is far likelier to be that file
# than a keystore is. Nothing here ignored it until 17 September 2026,
# found by a history sweep, and the timing is the point: the Stripe
# switch-on has somebody handling sk_live_ and whsec_ values by hand, and
# `stripe listen` prints a signing secret straight to a terminal next to
# an open editor. The Supabase CLI writes supabase/.env on its own.
#
# NOT .env.example -- a committed template with empty values is how the
# next person knows which names to set, so it is negated below.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.*.example

# Private keys, certificates and signing material, in every packaging the
# three shells use. None of these has ever been committed; these entries
# are for the same reason as the keystore ones.
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
*.key
*.cer
*.der
*.mobileprovision
id_rsa
id_ed25519

# Registry and machine credentials, which land in a repo by accident more
# often than by intent: `npm config set //registry…:_authToken` writes an
# .npmrc wherever it is run.
.npmrc
.netrc

# Service-account and mobile platform configuration that carries project
# secrets. google-services.json and GoogleService-Info.plist are written
# by the Firebase console into the native project folders, which are
# themselves ignored -- but not if somebody drops one at the root first.
service-account*.json
*-service-account.json
google-services.json
GoogleService-Info.plist

# A real lecture transcript, pasted in to run scripts/measure-summary-depth.mjs.
# It is a student's content and a lecturer's copyrighted delivery, and the
# script takes it as a path argument, so the obvious place to put one is the
# repository root. Ignored for the same reason as the keystore: the normal way
# work gets committed here is `git add -A`.
transcript.txt

.archive-test-tmp/
# e2e journey artifacts (per-run)
e2e/.state.json
e2e/test-results/

# c8 writes one JSON file per forked test process into coverage/tmp on
# every `npm run test:coverage`. Thirty of them were committed by PR #50
# and carried through two more, because `git add -A` is how work gets
# committed here and nothing was ignoring them -- the same mechanism as
# the keystore entries above, with a harmless payload instead of a
# catastrophic one. They are pure build output: the gate reads the
# `text` reporter's stdout, not these files.
coverage/

# THE LOCAL READ — scripts/read-asap.mjs writes ASAP essay text in full,
# and it is the only unredacted output anywhere in this project. The
# script REFUSES a path inside the repository, so this is the second
# line rather than the first: it covers the operator who passes --out
# and lands somewhere the fence allows but a later `git add -A` would
# still reach. Reading the corpus is permitted; redistributing it is
# not, and a commit is redistribution that cannot be taken back.
asap-read.md
*asap-read*.md
